principles.finding:
Access tokens expire 1 hour after they are issued. Every access token comes with a refresh token. Sent to the same token endpoint with grant_type=refresh_token, it returns a NEW access token and a NEW refresh token without involving the user.
Refresh tokens are single-use: once used, the old one is dead, and losing the latest one forces re-authorization. They stay valid until the user revokes them or uninstalls the integration.
Horus recommends:
- refresh a few minutes before expiry;
- on a 401, refresh once and retry;
- serialise refreshes per connection (two concurrent refreshes with the same token make one of them fail);
- persist the new pair before using it;
- encrypt tokens at rest.
No revocation endpoint and no absolute refresh-token expiry are documented.

A Horus Folder is the accounting record of one company: its configuration, chart of accounts, daybooks and entries. It carries Legislation, VatNumber, Language and the WithAnalytics and WithAssets module flags.
Endpoints that address an object by its own Id, or by a parent other than the folder, take no FolderId:
- the GET *.info reads;
- POST cost-schemas.info and cost-sections.info;
- companies.update, accounts.update, daybooks.update;
- ibans.list (CompanyId);
- book-entries.documents.new and .annexes.new (BookEntryId);
- the two DELETE endpoints;
- matching.next-number.* (Id).
invoices.sales.outstanding.info is ambiguous: the page text and the guide say the FolderId is required, but its only other query parameter is an optional, unnamed 'Id'.

No endpoint can be paged. Across all 72, the only query parameters are Id, BookEntryId, BookEntryNumber and UseAlternateHeading. No request body has a page, size, offset, cursor or limit field, no response carries a total, and none of the 15 guides mentions paging. (D) Lists are therefore expected to return the full Data array. Volume is held down only by:
- mandatory windows (month/year periods on the history endpoints, CreatedAfter/CreatedBefore on documents.list);
- ModifiedAfter incremental sync on 3 endpoints;
- gzip, which the guide recommends 'for large ledgers'.
Whether the server caps or truncates large results is not documented (?).

Each level uses a different set of language codes:
- license and user: FR, NL;
- folder: FR, NL, DE, XX;
- company: FR, NL, DE, EN, ES, PT, XX;
- companies.new LanguageCode: a free string with no enum (the guide example uses 'FR').
The country list labels English as 'Xx'. AlternateHeading (via UseAlternateHeading) gives headings in a second language. Enum labels are mostly in French or Dutch. The authorization pages exist in French (/fr/) and Dutch (/nl/).

The API reference has no invoice read model: no endpoint lists invoices, and apart from the open-items view of sales invoices none reads an invoice as such. One book entry can be read by Id with GET /book-entries.info, which takes only a query Id (no FolderId, DaybookId or month window). The Reading accounting data guide points to it for 'the full detail of one entry', but in the reference it returns the same object as one book-entries.list element:
- header: DocumentNo, Confirmed, Verified, EntryType, OperationType, Date, LastModifiedDate;
- CompanyDetail: company reference, DueDate, PaymentDate, TotalAmount, TurnoverAmount, TaxAmount, discount;
- {Type, Id} references to Folder, Daybook, the main Document and the AccountHistories lines.
Lines, line-level VAT and analytics are not embedded, annexes are not listed, and the endpoint is not one of the 4 sideloading endpoints. So line detail needs GET /account-histories.info per referenced line, or account-histories.list with Filter.BookEntryId inside a month window (D). Sets of entries are read through book-entries.list (FolderId, one DaybookId and a month window are required) or, as lines, through account-histories.list. Only sales invoices have an open-items view (invoices.sales.outstanding.list/info). Purchases have only suppliers.outstanding and account-histories.list with NotMatchedOnly, both at ledger-line level.

connector_conventions.rule:
Horus write lines use two non-negative columns, Debit and Credit (misc operations), or one Amount (bank and financial operations). Invoices use positive amounts plus a Type (0 invoice, 1 credit note): a refund sent with a negative total is multiplied by −1 as a whole document; mixed-sign documents and invoices with a negative total are rejected (proposal). Convert Chift signed amounts to that shape; for example, a misc-op amount that is positive goes to Credit and a negative one to Debit. On read, normalise Debit/Credit and signed TotalAmount (for example LinkedElements TotalAmount -210) to each Chift route's convention. The sign of credit-note amounts on read is undocumented.
Outstanding items use one side-based rule in every client and supplier path (primary, reconciliation lines, fallbacks), keyed on the endpoint or concentration account the line was read from, not on AccountType:
- lines read from the customer side (customers.outstanding, customer concentration accounts): amount = Debit − Credit; residual = Σ(Debit − Credit);
- lines read from the supplier side (suppliers.outstanding, supplier concentration accounts): amount = Credit − Debit; residual = Σ(Credit − Debit);
- rows of invoices.sales.outstanding.list: sign from TransactionType (0 sale invoice +, 1 sale credit note −, 2 payment −) rather than the native sign; miscellaneous-operation rows (3) keep the raw sign.
A line with an unexpected AccountType is kept and logged. Positive therefore means 'owed': receivable for clients, payable for suppliers (balance view, D5, proposal). Partly matched residuals go to the oldest due dates first (proposal; pro-rata is the alternative). Open question to Chift (C5): balance view, or the document view Chift uses for invoice due_dates.

Check Horus's documented rules before calling:
- TotalAmount = BaseAmount + TaxAmount; line totals equal the header within the agreed rounding tolerance (invoice_correction), larger differences rejected; each line's tax within a tolerance of base × rate;
- the VAT combination is in the folder's list for the invoice direction (appendix C);
- misc operations balance on the normalised (converted, rounded) amounts that are actually sent;
- cost lines add up to the line's BaseAmount (invoices) or Debit/Credit amount (misc operations);
- every analytic section is in the folder cache, active (422 otherwise) and in a List-type schema (Free schemas are not accepted in v1);
- no more than 8 analytic axes;
- the date is not in a closed period (closure dates read fresh);
- the file type is supported;
- no other company already has the VAT number.
This lets the connector return a clear Chift 4xx rather than an opaque Horus 400.
